   "reference": {
    "title": "INPC (Shimada) classification, MKI & INRGSS risk stratification",
    "intro": "INPC assigns Favourable vs Unfavourable histology by integrating Schwannian stroma content, neuroblastic differentiation, Mitosis-Karyorrhexis Index (MKI), and patient age (< 18 months, 18–60 months, >= 60 months). Evaluate pre-therapy tissue whenever available.",
    "note": "Photomicrographs are from Wikimedia Commons under open Creative Commons licences. ExpertPath pages open with your own ExpertPath login; the app holds none of their images.",
    "groups": [
     {
      "heading": "INPC age × differentiation × MKI rules & molecular markers",
      "points": [
       "Neuroblastoma < 18 months: Poorly differentiated or differentiating with Low (< 2%) or Intermediate (2–4%) MKI = Favourable; Undifferentiated or High MKI (> 4%) = Unfavourable.",
       "Neuroblastoma 18–60 months: Only Differentiating with Low MKI (< 2%) = Favourable; all others = Unfavourable. Age >= 60 months (5 years): always Unfavourable.",
       "Ganglioneuroma (maturing/mature) and Ganglioneuroblastoma intermixed = Favourable. Ganglioneuroblastoma nodular is stratified by applying INPC to the neuroblastic nodule.",
       "Key biomarkers: PHOX2B / synaptophysin / chromogranin / NB84 confirm lineage; MYCN amplification, 1p/11q segmental chromosomal aberrations (SCAs), ALK and ATRX drive risk stratification."
      ],
      "links": [
       {
